# Label fixture for tests/bench_rag_flags.py
#
# One JSON object per line. Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored, so this
# file doubles as the schema and as the load target.
#
# STATUS: EMPTY, awaiting the corpus. No row is present because a label must name a
# document that exists in the corpus, and both corpus layers are unavailable right now:
# the playbook layer needs pypdf, and the case layer needs the redacted production
# export. A hand-written label for a document that does not exist would produce a
# guaranteed miss and a meaningless Recall of zero, which is worse than no number.
#
# Fields, per plan section 2.1:
#
#   query_id     stable key. The full-width twin shares it, distinguished by `variant`.
#   query        the exact string sent. The harness never rewrites it.
#   lang         "en" or "id".
#   variant      "ascii" or "fullwidth". The harness derives the full-width twin with
#                str.translate, so the pair differs only in encoding.
#   target_text  "ascii" or "fullwidth", whether the TARGET DOCUMENT's own text contains
#                full-width characters. Drives the case A / case B split in plan 5.5.
#   source       the corpus label the target was ingested under, e.g. "cases",
#                "pdf:NIST.SP.800-61r3", or "text".
#   doc_key      the document identity used for the parent id derivation, WITHOUT the
#                source prefix:
#                  cases          -> the case_id
#                  pdf:<stem>     -> "<file basename>#p<page>"
#                  text           -> the caller-chosen key, e.g. "text0"
#                The harness resolves doc_id via rag_store._parent_id(source, doc_key),
#                which is deterministic. A single-chunk document has no parent, so such
#                a row supplies `chunk_id` instead.
#   child_seqs   which children carry the evidence. Drives child attribution and the
#                head / middle / tail classification.
#   band         "short" (<=1200 runes), "medium" (1201-3600), "long" (>3600).
#   position     "head" (child 0), "middle", "tail", or "n/a" for a single-chunk doc.
#   top_k        optional, default config.rag.top_k.
#   recall_k     optional, default config.rag.max_candidates.
#
# Example shape, for a multi-chunk case document. Values are placeholders, not labels:
#
# {"query_id":"case-0001","query":"ssh brute force on the mail relay",
#  "lang":"en","variant":"ascii","target_text":"ascii","band":"long","position":"middle",
#  "relevant":[{"source":"cases","doc_key":"<case_id>","child_seqs":[3],
#               "note":"the verdict line that answers this"}]}
#
# Minimum set, per plan section 1.3: 9 band-by-position cells with at least 5 rows each,
# plus at least 12 full-width pairs whose query_id matches an ascii row.
#
# To build the corpus first, on a host with pypdf and the model cache:
#   BLUETEAM_RAG_ENABLED=true BLUETEAM_RAG_DB=/tmp/bench/rag.db \
#   BLUETEAM_RAG_ALLOW_DOWNLOAD=false \
#   python3 -c "import asyncio,sys; sys.path.insert(0,'.'); \
#   from mcp_server.tools import rag_kb; \
#   print(asyncio.run(rag_kb.blueteam_rag_ingest.__wrapped__( \
#   rag_kb.RagIngestInput(source='pdf', path='resource/ir_playbooks/NIST.SP.800-61r3.pdf'))))"
#
# Then read parent ids and child seqs back out of the store to fill `doc_key` and
# `child_seqs` before hand-labelling position.
